{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-cologne-perfume-and-the-cathedral/audio/main-754338d1c6be.mp3","durationSec":333.071,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.3,"text":"Imagine standing in Cologne after building work on the cathedral had stopped."},{"start":4.94,"end":7.52,"text":"You look up at the cathedral, but it isn't finished."},{"start":8,"end":10.74,"text":"A huge wooden crane sits on one tower."},{"start":11.44,"end":18.8,"text":"This half-built church raises a simple question: why would anyone start such an enormous building and then leave it?"},{"start":19.3,"end":22.72,"text":"To answer that, we first need to know why it was started."},{"start":23.38,"end":25.2,"text":"The site was already religious."},{"start":25.58,"end":31.92,"text":"In the 6th century, after Roman rule ended, Merovingian kings built a church for a bishop there."},{"start":32.64,"end":36.54,"text":"Centuries later, Cologne's archbishops wanted something much bigger."},{"start":37.1,"end":40.34,"text":"They built the Gothic cathedral and new city walls together."},{"start":41.02,"end":47.62,"text":"Their aim was to promote Cologne as a \"Holy\" city and as the capital of their Electorate, the region they ruled."},{"start":48.32,"end":53.22,"text":"So the cathedral was about faith, but it was also about power and image."},{"start":53.82,"end":57.3,"text":"Construction began on 15 August 1248."},{"start":57.9,"end":63.36,"text":"By 1322, the eastern end, called the choir, was finished and consecrated."},{"start":63.82,"end":67.64,"text":"That means it was officially made holy and could be used for worship."},{"start":68.48,"end":70.5,"text":"Much of the early money came from pilgrims."},{"start":71.22,"end":75.64,"text":"These were travellers who came to visit the Shrine of the Three Kings inside the cathedral."},{"start":76.52,"end":79.46,"text":"Think of the cathedral like a business that depends on visitors."},{"start":79.94,"end":82.94,"text":"When many visitors arrive, the work can continue."},{"start":83.64,"end":88.82,"text":"The key point for the rest of the story is that the building relied on money from outside the city."},{"start":89.54,"end":93.9,"text":"Many people assume the cathedral took so long because medieval builders were slow."},{"start":94.58,"end":95.58,"text":"Look at the dates, though."},{"start":96.02,"end":98.9,"text":"The choir was finished in about 74 years."},{"start":99.44,"end":102.74,"text":"The long delay came later, and it had clear causes."},{"start":103.44,"end":105.14,"text":"The first problem was money."},{"start":105.14,"end":111.06,"text":"Income from pilgrims declined, and by 1473 the funding had stopped."},{"start":111.68,"end":116.84,"text":"In 1473, work stopped with the south tower built up to the level of the bells."},{"start":117.56,"end":122.58,"text":"A large crane was left standing on it, and it stayed there for 400 years."},{"start":123.36,"end":125.12,"text":"The second problem was war."},{"start":125.7,"end":130.3,"text":"In 1474, Cologne needed its craftsmen for something more urgent."},{"start":130.88,"end":134.36,"text":"The city had to strengthen its defences against the Duke of Burgundy."},{"start":134.36,"end":138.74,"text":"When your city is under threat, walls matter more than towers."},{"start":138.74,"end":141.82,"text":"The third problem was religious change."},{"start":142.44,"end":145.24,"text":"The cathedral had also earned money from indulgences."},{"start":145.98,"end":148.54,"text":"These were payments connected to the forgiveness of sins."},{"start":149.3,"end":153.24,"text":"The Reformation challenged this practice, and that income was lost."},{"start":153.88,"end":157.6,"text":"By around 1560, construction had stopped completely."},{"start":158.4,"end":160.78,"text":"Notice how each cause made the others worse."},{"start":160.78,"end":165.08,"text":"Fewer pilgrims meant less money, and war took away the workers."},{"start":165.82,"end":169.08,"text":"After that, the Reformation removed another source of income."},{"start":169.72,"end":171.44,"text":"The story did not end there."},{"start":172.06,"end":177.56,"text":"Efforts to restart began around 1814, and work started again in 1823."},{"start":178.2,"end":181.8,"text":"However, proper funding only arrived in the 1840s."},{"start":182.22,"end":188.1,"text":"The builders completed the long main hall, the side arms and the towers in the 19th century."},{"start":188.1,"end":190.18,"text":"They used the medieval plans to do it."},{"start":190.18,"end":192.68,"text":"Now move forward to 1709."},{"start":193.34,"end":197.36,"text":"The cathedral was still unfinished, and the old crane was still on the tower."},{"start":197.94,"end":203.3,"text":"In that year, an Italian named Johann Maria Farina created a new fragrance in Cologne."},{"start":203.94,"end":206.22,"text":"He made it from citrus oils and alcohol."},{"start":206.88,"end":210.94,"text":"He was inspired by the memory of an Italian spring morning after rain."},{"start":211.44,"end":213.02,"text":"This is an interesting twist."},{"start":213.66,"end":216.8,"text":"One of Cologne's most famous products came from an outsider."},{"start":216.8,"end":221,"text":"Its feeling came from Italy, but its name came from Cologne."},{"start":221.84,"end":225.3,"text":"Every bottle that travelled abroad carried the city's name with it."},{"start":225.3,"end":228.08,"text":"Famous customers made this effect much stronger."},{"start":228.08,"end":235.34,"text":"Napoleon, the writer Goethe, Queen Victoria and Empress Elisabeth all used Farina's Eau de Cologne."},{"start":236,"end":238.76,"text":"Napoleon is said to have used two bottles a day."},{"start":239.24,"end":243.34,"text":"When powerful and admired people use a product, others want it too."},{"start":243.34,"end":248.2,"text":"In this way, the perfume made Cologne world-famous."},{"start":248.2,"end":252.16,"text":"At first, a cathedral and a perfume seem to have nothing in common."},{"start":252.84,"end":255.18,"text":"One is made of stone and the other is a scent."},{"start":255.84,"end":258.3,"text":"But both stories follow a similar pattern."},{"start":258.96,"end":261.6,"text":"First, both depended on people from outside."},{"start":261.6,"end":266.44,"text":"Pilgrims' money built the cathedral, and the work stopped when that money stopped."},{"start":267.06,"end":271.28,"text":"An Italian created the perfume, and famous foreigners spread its name."},{"start":271.28,"end":275.66,"text":"In both cases, Cologne's fame was built partly by others."},{"start":275.66,"end":280.28,"text":"Second, both stories are about keeping old work alive."},{"start":281,"end":285.64,"text":"The cathedral was finished with medieval plans, centuries after the first stone was laid."},{"start":286.32,"end":291.76,"text":"The Farina company was founded in 1709 and is the world's oldest fragrance maker."},{"start":292.48,"end":296.92,"text":"Farina family descendants still make the original Eau de Cologne in the city today."},{"start":296.92,"end":303.88,"text":"Since 1723, the company's headquarters has been the Farina House, which is now the Fragrance Museum."},{"start":304.82,"end":309.2,"text":"So let's return to the old idea that medieval builders were simply slow."},{"start":309.76,"end":311.76,"text":"They finished the choir in a lifetime."},{"start":312.46,"end":321.22,"text":"The cathedral waited because pilgrim income declined, craftsmen were needed for the city's defences, and the Reformation cut off money from indulgences."},{"start":321.22,"end":326.92,"text":"Cologne's story is less about slowness and more about dependence and patience."},{"start":327.72,"end":332.78,"text":"The city relied on pilgrims and customers, and it kept returning to unfinished work."}],"words":[{"text":"Imagine","start":0,"end":0.5},{"text":"standing","start":0.5,"end":0.96},{"text":"in","start":0.96,"end":1.32},{"text":"Cologne","start":1.32,"end":